#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119570 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b34272b7-a7ad-5a58-974e-54cca957d06e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of the Plane Perpendicular To a Given Vector"}] :question/id #uuid "01f32313-178c-5626-91af-110907202416" :question/math-academy-id "e-4936" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(9,-2,3)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $\\mathbf{n}=3\\mathbf{i}-4\\mathbf{j}+7\\mathbf{k}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=\\langle 9,-2,3 \\rangle$ and the normal vector of the plane is $\\mathbf{n}=\\langle 3,-4,7 \\rangle.$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨3,-4,7⟩ & =⟨9,-2,3⟩\\cdot ⟨3,-4,7⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨3,-4,7⟩ & =56.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $\\mathbf{r}=\\langle x,y,z \\rangle$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨3,-4,7⟩ & =56 \\\\ 3x-4y+7z & =56 \\\\ 3x-4y+7z-56 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119571 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2173739c-cd59-58ed-a265-8ee88c982ae0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of the Plane Perpendicular To a Given Line"}] :question/id #uuid "d92eb242-104f-55cd-82dc-7620e2f4e636" :question/math-academy-id "e-4937" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(-1,4,8)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n$\\dfrac{x+3}{-5}=\\dfrac{y-1}{3}=\\dfrac{z+2}{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=\\langle -1,4,8 \\rangle.$ From the canonical equation, we find that the line is parallel to the vector $\\langle -5,3,2 \\rangle.$ So, the normal vector of the plane is \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}=\\langle -5,3,2 \\rangle.\n$$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-5,3,2⟩ & =⟨-1,4,8⟩\\cdot ⟨-5,3,2⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-5,3,2⟩ & =33\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $\\mathbf{r}=\\langle x,y,z \\rangle$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨-5,3,2⟩ & =33 \\\\ -5x+3y+2z & =33 \\\\ -5x+3y+2z-33 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119572 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "80c443e5-a0b2-5aae-ac8d-27449a8049a3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Plane Given Its Cartesian Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "aa7c3400-9bc0-5f3a-84e5-f3cc2bb87f5d" :question/math-academy-id "e-4938" :question/problem "Given the Cartesian equation $2x-y+z+13=0$ of the plane $\\Pi,$ find an equation of $\\Pi$ in the form $(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p})\\cdot\\mathbf{n}=0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, from the coefficients in equation of the plane, we get that the normal vector of $\\Pi$ is $\\mathbf{n}=\\langle 2,-1,1 \\rangle.$\n\nNow, we solve the Cartesian equation of $\\Pi$ for $z\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-y+z+13 & =0 \\\\ z & =-2x+y-13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we put, say, $x=0$ and $y=0,$ we get $z=-13.$ So, the point $P(0,0,-13)$ lies on $\\Pi.$ Note that the position vector of $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=\\langle 0,0,-13 \\rangle.$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ (r-⟨0,0,-13⟩)\\cdot ⟨2,-1,1⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119573 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0efe5b11-2c87-58e9-bf7c-e1eecb07adf2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of the Plane Parallel To Two Vectors"}] :question/id #uuid "8a9e1070-0ca0-5f24-ac89-999c9271a91b" :question/math-academy-id "e-4939" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(-3,2,-6)$ and is parallel to the vectors $\\mathbf{v_1}=\\langle -3, 5, 2 \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{v_2}=\\langle 1, 3, -2 \\rangle.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=\\langle -3,2,-6 \\rangle.$\n\nNow, we need to find the normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of $\\mathbf{v_1}$ and $\\mathbf{v_2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={v}_{1}\\times {v}_{2} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\times [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -3 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -2\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-16i-4j-14k \\\\  & =⟨-16,-4,-14⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-16,-4,-14⟩ & =⟨-3,2,-6⟩\\cdot ⟨-16,-4,-14⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-16,-4,-14⟩ & =124\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $\\mathbf{r}=\\langle x,y,z \\rangle$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨-16,-4,-14⟩ & =124 \\\\ -16x-4y-14z & =124 \\\\ -16x-4y-14z-124 & =0 \\\\ 8x+2y+7z+62 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317151 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b34272b7-a7ad-5a58-974e-54cca957d06e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of the Plane Perpendicular To a Given Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74e40a26-493d-5b63-b2a7-b0c0857a63c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x-3y-z+13=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317153} {:answer/id #uuid "96600223-6ac2-5aae-af5f-d339a3bf95ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x+3y-z-11=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317154} {:answer/id #uuid "8fad3f9f-dd27-58db-96a2-20d9a9edd43f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x+4y-z-7=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317155} {:answer/id #uuid "cfd52b89-44ba-5ac6-aa3e-ee8390f035fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x-4y-z+9=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317156} {:answer/id #uuid "83c33c72-a071-596b-90b3-cb3ce07d269b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+3y-4z-17=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317157}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8fad3f9f-dd27-58db-96a2-20d9a9edd43f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x+4y-z-7=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317155} :answer-field/id #uuid "39431669-f8b7-5624-822e-3855a0a51429" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317152}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "03ce23ca-0088-57fb-b4ca-21f59a96ca63" :question/math-academy-id "q-127967" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(-2,3,-1)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $n=3i+4j-k.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $p=⟨-2,3,-1⟩$ and the normal vector of the plane is $n=⟨3,4,-1⟩.$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨3,4,-1⟩ & =⟨-2,3,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨3,4,-1⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨3,4,-1⟩ & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨3,4,-1⟩ & =7 \\\\ 3x+4y-z & =7 \\\\ 3x+4y-z-7 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388290 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2173739c-cd59-58ed-a265-8ee88c982ae0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of the Plane Perpendicular To a Given Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e2594059-827b-55b5-8208-d8a00afb06f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-y+z-6=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388292} {:answer/id #uuid "773ac187-9f1a-5da9-b359-cb81e454cded" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-x-y+z+1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388293} {:answer/id #uuid "45896f8a-7944-531c-893e-522a8af1d878" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-y-z+3=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388294} {:answer/id #uuid "0f03d236-ebd3-5571-9e7b-706d5d055a76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y-z+6=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388295} {:answer/id #uuid "f8f4ca43-67ad-54b5-88d2-654580ef9be5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y+z-3=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388296}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f03d236-ebd3-5571-9e7b-706d5d055a76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y-z+6=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388295} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3cf2ec3-b08f-5c28-b812-c14f968dc777" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388291}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "73a51566-e903-5429-a833-3a965966f7dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-128376" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(1,-5,2)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n${\\begin{aligned}x=3+t \\\\ y=t \\\\ z=7-t\\end{aligned}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨1,-5,2⟩.$ From the parametrical equation, we find that the line is parallel to the vector $⟨1,1,-1⟩.$ So, the normal vector of the plane is \n\n$$\nn=⟨1,1,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨1,1,-1⟩ & =⟨1,-5,2⟩\\cdot ⟨1,1,-1⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨1,1,-1⟩ & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨1,1,-1⟩ & =-6 \\\\ x+y-z & =-6 \\\\ x+y-z+6 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388297 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "80c443e5-a0b2-5aae-ac8d-27449a8049a3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Plane Given Its Cartesian Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b591e97-568e-54e1-b2ff-3b25d6f8a868" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r-⟨0,-3,0⟩)\\cdot ⟨7,-1,0⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388299} {:answer/id #uuid "8bfbc34b-25a5-5d23-bc9c-f80e7085e552" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r-⟨0,9,0⟩)\\cdot ⟨-7,0,1⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388300} {:answer/id #uuid "1f0a58eb-4f3d-598b-8f9e-aaeb7207b6f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r-⟨0,6,0⟩)\\cdot ⟨0,7,1⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388301} {:answer/id #uuid "990652fd-70a4-5d37-8d07-9f27ede2f633" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r-⟨0,3,0⟩)\\cdot ⟨7,1,0⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388302} {:answer/id #uuid "75ce8e92-2131-5643-828a-adff4a01a923" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r-⟨0,-6,0⟩)\\cdot ⟨7,0,1⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388303}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "990652fd-70a4-5d37-8d07-9f27ede2f633" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r-⟨0,3,0⟩)\\cdot ⟨7,1,0⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388302} :answer-field/id #uuid "10b864a4-1db5-53bd-a23e-5baed302ea88" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388298}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a275fd86-a21a-5ef1-ae5c-9df806e069b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-128400" :question/problem "Given the Cartesian equation $7x+y-3=0$ of the plane $Π$, find an equation of $Π$ in the form $(r-p)\\cdot n=0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, from the equation of the plane we get that the normal vector of $Π$ is $n=⟨7,1,0⟩.$\n\nNow, we solve the Cartesian equation of $Π$ for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7x+y-3 & =0 \\\\ y & =3-7x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we put, say, $x=0$, we get $y=3.$ Hence, the point $P(0,3,0)$ lies on $Π.$ Note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨0,3,0⟩.$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ (r-⟨0,3,0⟩)\\cdot ⟨7,1,0⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388304 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0efe5b11-2c87-58e9-bf7c-e1eecb07adf2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of the Plane Parallel To Two Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ed723e51-f1a8-57b1-8bcc-cbd523f467cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388306} {:answer/id #uuid "778b5a41-2ec5-55c3-b9ae-e8c86cf0ff20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388307}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ed723e51-f1a8-57b1-8bcc-cbd523f467cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388306} :answer-field/id #uuid "904b214a-ae22-5da8-80b3-34896b7bc41c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388305} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1393e66b-02fc-5824-8c21-c9d22567df56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388309}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1393e66b-02fc-5824-8c21-c9d22567df56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388309} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c61baff-428f-5056-b452-d1bac27bc5ce" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388308} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e575f31-f881-5e2d-8d93-b41a75073669" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "39" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388311}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e575f31-f881-5e2d-8d93-b41a75073669" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "39" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388311} :answer-field/id #uuid "35957583-1ed3-5158-8d03-b280590e6b8c" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388310}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "11d73e76-733c-5c1a-b58a-cd0a522da6dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-326312" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(-3,2,4)$ and is parallel to the vectors ${v}_{1}=⟨1,-2,-5⟩$ and ${v}_{2}=⟨0,1,-7⟩.$\n\n{{field-1}}\n$x\\,+$\n{{field-2}}\n$y+z\\,+$\n{{field-3}}\n$=0$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $p=⟨-3,2,4⟩.$\n\nNow, we need to find the normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={v}_{1}\\times {v}_{2} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]\\times [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -2 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -7\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =19i+7j+k \\\\  & =⟨19,7,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨19,7,1⟩ & =⟨-3,2,4⟩\\cdot ⟨19,7,1⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨19,7,1⟩ & =-39\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨19,7,1⟩ & =-39 \\\\ 19x+7y+z & =-39 \\\\ 19x+7y+z+39 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317186 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b34272b7-a7ad-5a58-974e-54cca957d06e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of the Plane Perpendicular To a Given Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd320510-1075-5e34-8eeb-c578ca849a56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x+y-5z+26=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317188} {:answer/id #uuid "6489f1d6-5bad-54b1-8041-e0b0f9e9e222" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3y-5z+24=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317189} {:answer/id #uuid "7d72cb60-ea74-5c35-8b5a-0618e41e1504" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3y+5z+26=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317190} {:answer/id #uuid "a1b80d53-2e9a-538d-9b63-efb1fb02a239" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3y-5z-26=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317191} {:answer/id #uuid "5c7a3741-4493-561e-8fef-e14d90b81345" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3y-5z+26=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317192}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c7a3741-4493-561e-8fef-e14d90b81345" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3y-5z+26=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317192} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b3cc707-a5f8-5ce6-958c-76852cfc90f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317187}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bc6b7572-6525-5f0a-acdf-da49eecac498" :question/math-academy-id "q-330498" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(1,-2,4)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $n=⟨0,3,-5⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $p=⟨1,-2,4⟩$ and the normal vector of the plane is $n=⟨0,3,-5⟩.$\n\nUsing the dot product we can find an equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨0,3,-5⟩ & =⟨1,-2,4⟩\\cdot ⟨0,3,-5⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨0,3,-5⟩ & =-26\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,3,-5⟩ & =-26 \\\\ 3y-5z & =-26 \\\\ 3y-5z+26 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388312 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b34272b7-a7ad-5a58-974e-54cca957d06e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of the Plane Perpendicular To a Given Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3772d1eb-4c9f-5d5b-b49f-8f0e29c2b36f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y-z-18=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388314} {:answer/id #uuid "124474af-26ba-5dda-98d6-37a32065d8e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+4y+z+5=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388315} {:answer/id #uuid "08c56b76-bc81-5a59-8402-593bf00cce7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5x+y-3z-13=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388316} {:answer/id #uuid "849db852-c8cb-556f-9638-60d492ba681a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5x-3z-13=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388317} {:answer/id #uuid "9293726e-4e2e-5cd8-bfdc-60c58b378cf6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+4y-z-13=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388318}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9293726e-4e2e-5cd8-bfdc-60c58b378cf6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+4y-z-13=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388318} :answer-field/id #uuid "c094f0b7-fb1e-5ddb-8ade-9f0e68866fda" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388313}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "19fdb04a-9367-5fe1-9cea-0be46faa4ebc" :question/math-academy-id "q-33067" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(5,0,-3)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $n=⟨2,4,-1⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $p=⟨5,0,-3⟩$ and the normal vector of the plane is $n=⟨2,4,-1⟩.$\n\nUsing the dot product we can find an equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨2,4,-1⟩ & =⟨5,0,-3⟩\\cdot ⟨2,4,-1⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨2,4,-1⟩ & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨2,4,-1⟩ & =13 \\\\ 2x+4y-z & =13 \\\\ 2x+4y-z-13 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384646 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0efe5b11-2c87-58e9-bf7c-e1eecb07adf2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of the Plane Parallel To Two Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55723dcb-605f-595d-8fae-1ee5436e7a81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15x-8y-3z-67=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384648} {:answer/id #uuid "42a8ae72-16c3-587a-a3e7-b75bffe928ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-5y+z-70=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384649} {:answer/id #uuid "803a1755-2134-5aa8-a86a-8015bacc22d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5x+y-67=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384650} {:answer/id #uuid "2bd5cb89-4f60-5887-afe3-a7c5a3523298" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15x-8y-3z+70=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384651} {:answer/id #uuid "31dcdae4-9b1f-53d5-a9d2-7fbe89fc944a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x-2y+3z=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384652}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "55723dcb-605f-595d-8fae-1ee5436e7a81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15x-8y-3z-67=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384648} :answer-field/id #uuid "362de8bd-fdfa-5aa6-b00e-5111d888298e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384647}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c5938084-81eb-59aa-b8c4-275f8497be94" :question/math-academy-id "q-33142" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the points $A(2,-5,1)$, $P(4,-2,3)$ and $Q(5,1,0).$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the position vectors of the points $A,P$ and $Q$ are $a=⟨2,-5,1⟩,\\,p=⟨4,-2,3⟩,$ and $q=⟨5,1,0⟩.$\n\nFirst, using the $3$ points we build $2$ vectors parallel to the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} & =\\overset{\\to }{AP} \\\\  & =⟨4,-2,3⟩-⟨2,-5,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,3,2⟩ \\\\ {v}_{2} & =\\overset{\\to }{AQ} \\\\  & =⟨5,1,0⟩-⟨2,-5,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,6,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow,  we need to find the normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={v}_{1}\\times {v}_{2} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\times [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 6 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-15i+8j+3k \\\\  & =⟨-15,8,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-15,8,3⟩ & =⟨4,-2,3⟩\\cdot ⟨-15,8,3⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-15,8,3⟩ & =-67\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨-15,8,3⟩ & =-67 \\\\ -15x+8y+3z & =-67 \\\\ -15x+8y+3z+67 & =0 \\\\ 15x-8y-3z-67 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388319 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2173739c-cd59-58ed-a265-8ee88c982ae0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of the Plane Perpendicular To a Given Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19fc3d16-e8e1-5339-bebe-dbfb05c66204" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x-5y+z+1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388321} {:answer/id #uuid "90c65b6b-1e8c-5704-b492-3ae24d0a7c53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3x+y+4z-1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388322} {:answer/id #uuid "199582ba-c325-595d-b2f4-33c4e5cdfdcf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x-5y+2z+1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388323} {:answer/id #uuid "ec30125d-b6ec-57f0-a7ed-21cb20936f89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5x+3y+2z+1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388324} {:answer/id #uuid "11d2e9ed-28d4-5d80-a01d-2565bfaf0a6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3x+4z+1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388325}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "199582ba-c325-595d-b2f4-33c4e5cdfdcf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x-5y+2z+1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388323} :answer-field/id #uuid "81bbdfc3-17f8-59b1-be4b-9724719cc071" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388320}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c43b9462-37b6-5478-904d-7307590edba1" :question/math-academy-id "q-33162" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(-3,0,4)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n$r=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨-3,0,4⟩.$ From the vectorial equation, we find that the line is parallel to the vector $⟨3,-5,2⟩.$ So, the normal vector of the plane is \n\n$$\nn=⟨3,-5,2⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨3,-5,2⟩ & =⟨-3,0,4⟩\\cdot ⟨3,-5,2⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨3,-5,2⟩ & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨3,-5,2⟩ & =-1 \\\\ 3x-5y+2z & =-1 \\\\ 3x-5y+2z+1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120170 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2173739c-cd59-58ed-a265-8ee88c982ae0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of the Plane Perpendicular To a Given Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "951c6a2b-706d-5fc0-a1db-31a994bfc62e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8x + 3y - z - 42 = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117541} {:answer/id #uuid "b162df82-2366-5bd5-a612-c1ed54ffbc67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2x + y + 2z = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117542} {:answer/id #uuid "fb4541cf-b761-55dd-8dce-1934399f0089" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x + 3y - z = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117543} {:answer/id #uuid "43586768-6810-5b8c-b59a-7cf0eb1247ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2x + y + 2z - 42 = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117544} {:answer/id #uuid "75b979ae-630b-5445-b684-42e7a983e382" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x + 3y - z - 42 = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117545}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "75b979ae-630b-5445-b684-42e7a983e382" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x + 3y - z - 42 = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117545} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4e271cb-b49f-584f-8191-ea123e7adc06" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117540}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b0dec6df-1301-5550-8bf9-7363cae85702" :question/math-academy-id "q-33165" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(8,3,-1)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n$\\frac{x+2}{4}=\\frac{y-1}{3}=\\frac{z-2}{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨8,3,-1⟩.$ From the canonical equation, we find that the line is parallel to the vector $⟨4,3,-1⟩.$ So, the normal vector of the plane is \n\n$$\nn=⟨4,3,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨4,3,-1⟩ & =⟨8,3,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨4,3,-1⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨4,3,-1⟩ & =42\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨4,3,-1⟩ & =42 \\\\ 4x+3y-z & =42 \\\\ 4x+3y-z-42 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388326 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2173739c-cd59-58ed-a265-8ee88c982ae0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of the Plane Perpendicular To a Given Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "75f97724-6015-5d4f-87ac-0be426a764c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388328}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "75f97724-6015-5d4f-87ac-0be426a764c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388328} :answer-field/id #uuid "822ca1cd-0722-519d-9885-9d8ed65c1c2a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388327} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e5806d8d-242f-5177-aaed-7461250dc45b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388330}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e5806d8d-242f-5177-aaed-7461250dc45b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388330} :answer-field/id #uuid "c6ba7856-4e39-520b-8fd7-e93a3a291acb" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388329} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43d948d7-d5d5-5723-b0e6-cf140ffb4b18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388332}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "43d948d7-d5d5-5723-b0e6-cf140ffb4b18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388332} :answer-field/id #uuid "737b3577-6bf4-51a3-8235-bb1a49a894db" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388331}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "180db9bd-178c-5bc0-8e09-b035c76a3c3e" :question/math-academy-id "q-336111" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(-3,4,1)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n${\\begin{aligned}x=-1+2t \\\\ y=5+3t \\\\ z=7-t\\end{aligned}.$\n\n{{field-1}}$\\,x\\,+\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n$y\\,-\\,z\\,-\\,$\n{{field-3}}\n$=0$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨-3,4,1⟩.$ From the parametrical equation, we find that the line is parallel to the vector $⟨2,3,-1⟩.$ So, the normal vector of the plane is \n\n$$\nn=⟨2,3,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨2,3,-1⟩ & =⟨-3,4,1⟩\\cdot ⟨2,3,-1⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨2,3,-1⟩ & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨2,3,-1⟩ & =5 \\\\ 2x+3y-z & =5 \\\\ 2x+3y-z-5 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388333 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2173739c-cd59-58ed-a265-8ee88c982ae0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of the Plane Perpendicular To a Given Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05b2db7f-2ae1-5b39-b4d8-554a15eee51d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4y+z-4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388335} {:answer/id #uuid "a253867a-ddb0-56d8-9ad9-21f2a16c1aa5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4y+z-3=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388336} {:answer/id #uuid "15b52294-a5b4-582b-aa07-7a85d3e4b93b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4y+2z-4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388337} {:answer/id #uuid "7281ddb0-fe67-5741-a26f-7de06840a772" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4y-z-4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388338} {:answer/id #uuid "50d6ccf2-85d5-50dc-aab3-938de324058c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+4y+z-4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388339}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "05b2db7f-2ae1-5b39-b4d8-554a15eee51d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4y+z-4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388335} :answer-field/id #uuid "1bec8bf2-432e-55fc-bd19-7d187da4a49a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388334}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5476e4b7-9d7c-5e18-af79-26ad89717789" :question/math-academy-id "q-336213" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(2,1,0)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n${\\begin{aligned}x=1 \\\\ y=-2+4t \\\\ z=3+t\\end{aligned}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨2,1,0⟩.$ From the parametrical equation, we find that the line is parallel to the vector $⟨0,4,1⟩.$ So, the normal vector of the plane is \n\n$$\nn=⟨0,4,1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨0,4,1⟩ & =⟨2,1,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,4,1⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨0,4,1⟩ & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,4,1⟩ & =4 \\\\ 4y+z & =4 \\\\ 4y+z-4 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388340 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "80c443e5-a0b2-5aae-ac8d-27449a8049a3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Plane Given Its Cartesian Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e4df9772-3f1b-50ef-82a6-bd28b0cf7e2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388342} {:answer/id #uuid "5e800bf1-d28a-5a53-a920-d469452ec071" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388343}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e800bf1-d28a-5a53-a920-d469452ec071" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388343} :answer-field/id #uuid "66b2c1da-3311-5fce-8876-7111c97ae2b4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388341} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d163a137-3ef1-5244-b287-5634e8b6882d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388345}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d163a137-3ef1-5244-b287-5634e8b6882d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388345} :answer-field/id #uuid "33ebb38b-e5f7-56c8-9df7-d453025c72f1" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388344} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22c3b04b-3341-55b5-905b-1537aa5d1989" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388347}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "22c3b04b-3341-55b5-905b-1537aa5d1989" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388347} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8c9445d-da75-541b-a9ab-a99ea221973f" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388346}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8a835807-097c-577a-9751-b96b199e051b" :question/math-academy-id "q-336220" :question/problem "Given the Cartesian equation $3x+y+2z+4=0$ of the plane $Π$, find an equation of $Π$ in the form $(r-p)\\cdot n=0.$\n\n$(r-⟨0,0,$\n{{field-1}}\n$⟩)\\cdot ⟨$ \n{{field-2}}\n$,1\\,,$\n{{field-3}}\n$⟩=0$" :question/worked-solution "First, from the equation of the plane we get that the normal vector of $Π$ is $n=⟨3,1,2⟩.$\n\nNow, we solve the Cartesian equation of $Π$ for $z$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+y+2z+4 & =0 \\\\ 2z & =-3x-y-4 \\\\ z & =-\\frac{3}{2}x-\\frac{1}{2}y-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we put $x=0$ and $y=0$, we get $z=-2.$ Hence, the point $P(0,0,-2)$ lies on $Π.$ Note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨0,0,-2⟩.$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ (r-⟨0,0,-2⟩)\\cdot ⟨3,1,2⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388348 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "80c443e5-a0b2-5aae-ac8d-27449a8049a3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Plane Given Its Cartesian Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53e214a7-efc7-522f-9b20-60311710ef8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388350}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "53e214a7-efc7-522f-9b20-60311710ef8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388350} :answer-field/id #uuid "3347e566-35a5-50a3-837d-05ebc285bf1c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388349} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5288c902-d56c-5ce1-8bee-d4b43b5daaa9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388352}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5288c902-d56c-5ce1-8bee-d4b43b5daaa9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388352} :answer-field/id #uuid "657fcdb9-feaa-5ed7-aa02-89fdfff374f3" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388351} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91965d3f-b50a-57df-9e23-28c61ef3ee7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388354}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91965d3f-b50a-57df-9e23-28c61ef3ee7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388354} :answer-field/id #uuid "de0fe834-bf1d-556d-9fd9-5ed7a2506cab" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388353}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e29c766d-a204-551a-be78-6b62beba7d30" :question/math-academy-id "q-336225" :question/problem "Given the Cartesian equation $5x-7y+4z+20=0$ of the plane $Π$, find an equation of $Π$ in the form $(r-p)\\cdot n=0.$\n\n$(r-⟨0,0,$\n{{field-1}}\n$⟩)\\cdot ⟨5,$\n{{field-2}}\n,\n{{field-3}}\n$⟩=0$" :question/worked-solution "First, from the equation of the plane we get that the normal vector of $Π$ is $n=⟨5,-7,4⟩.$\n\nNow, we solve the Cartesian equation of $Π$ for $z$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x-7y+4z+20 & =0 \\\\ z & =-\\frac{5}{4}x+\\frac{7}{4}y-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we put $x=0$ and $y=0$, we get $z=-5.$ Hence, the point $P(0,0,-5)$ lies on $Π.$ Note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨0,0,-5⟩.$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ (r-⟨0,0,-5⟩)\\cdot ⟨5,-7,4⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384668 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "80c443e5-a0b2-5aae-ac8d-27449a8049a3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Plane Given Its Cartesian Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d0eb7ad-9b50-5dc6-8b87-7f76312128ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384670}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d0eb7ad-9b50-5dc6-8b87-7f76312128ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384670} :answer-field/id #uuid "b27f8f14-201a-514c-be9b-ed56526f88d6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384669} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e32d4500-7940-5053-86bb-fbf9704e32fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384672}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e32d4500-7940-5053-86bb-fbf9704e32fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384672} :answer-field/id #uuid "40f9f4ed-dcee-5879-891b-4aeea776472c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384671} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c8a3a99-7496-5337-b5d9-580ecd2bdac2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384674}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3c8a3a99-7496-5337-b5d9-580ecd2bdac2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384674} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7ce9af2-3886-50d4-8ccd-8959a18d4900" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384673}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e72d0f67-c2b4-5692-91cc-01e26d063987" :question/math-academy-id "q-336228" :question/problem "Given the Cartesian equation $5x+2y+z-4=0$ of the plane $Π$, find an equation of $Π$ in the form $(r-p)\\cdot n=0.$\n\n$(r-⟨0,0,$\n{{field-1}}\n$⟩)\\cdot ⟨$\n{{field-2}} $,\\,2\\,,$\n{{field-3}}\n$⟩=0$" :question/worked-solution "First, from the equation of the plane we get that the normal vector of $Π$ is $n=⟨5,2,1⟩.$\n\nNow, we solve the Cartesian equation of $Π$ for $z$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x+2y+z-4 & =0 \\\\ z & =-5x-2y+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we put $x=0$ and $y=0$, we get $z=4.$ Hence, the point $P(0,0,4)$ lies on $Π.$ Note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨0,0,4⟩.$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ (r-⟨0,0,4⟩)\\cdot ⟨5,\\,2\\,,1⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388355 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "80c443e5-a0b2-5aae-ac8d-27449a8049a3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Plane Given Its Cartesian Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "df17996d-5bda-5fb6-948d-2259f9ade96a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388357}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df17996d-5bda-5fb6-948d-2259f9ade96a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388357} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ecca9ad-3016-56d2-93c3-072fb1ce2ff1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388356} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22dd3d82-bfef-548e-b8e1-1470fac71c64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388359}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "22dd3d82-bfef-548e-b8e1-1470fac71c64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388359} :answer-field/id #uuid "17945930-eb69-5477-91ee-84c5615f76a4" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388358} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b6bf230-3342-5f4c-a57a-e0a78d873669" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388361}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b6bf230-3342-5f4c-a57a-e0a78d873669" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388361} :answer-field/id #uuid "302ab8b7-2c4e-5a79-92a5-5ccbacd014ef" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388360}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "34fb864d-1d68-5d55-91b9-7781b21f48c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-336239" :question/problem "Given the Cartesian equation $9x-3y+6=0$ of the plane $Π$, find an equation of $Π$ in the form $(r-p)\\cdot n=0.$\n\n$(r-⟨0,$\n{{field-1}}$\\,,0$\n\n$⟩)\\cdot ⟨$\n{{field-2}}\n,\n{{field-3}}\n$,0⟩=0$" :question/worked-solution "First, from the equation of the plane we get that the normal vector of $Π$ is $n=⟨9,-3,0⟩.$\n\nNow, we solve the Cartesian equation of $Π$ for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9x-3y+6 & =0 \\\\ -3y & =-9x-6 \\\\ y & =3x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we put, say, $x=0$, we get $y=2.$ Hence, the point $P(0,2,0)$ lies on $Π.$ Note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨0,2,0⟩.$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ (r-⟨0,2,0⟩)\\cdot ⟨9,-3,0⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186330671 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0efe5b11-2c87-58e9-bf7c-e1eecb07adf2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of the Plane Parallel To Two Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f7d4c5e8-f936-53a9-bc5f-3cca97c3b2c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330673}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f7d4c5e8-f936-53a9-bc5f-3cca97c3b2c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330673} :answer-field/id #uuid "18d0d219-ec4f-53bb-97f0-b1c5bd529520" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330672} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f369a1e-a1c1-580a-b60d-5664c6315eb4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330675}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f369a1e-a1c1-580a-b60d-5664c6315eb4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330675} :answer-field/id #uuid "842469ef-6b3f-52d4-b990-df6cd1e2c154" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330674} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c637def1-cd89-5a2f-83b5-1ab8c3027aad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330677}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c637def1-cd89-5a2f-83b5-1ab8c3027aad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330677} :answer-field/id #uuid "c362e1f6-816b-5c65-bc37-a382d6255263" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330676}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bc0bcd5d-4d1d-5e96-9f96-539ff833a355" :question/math-academy-id "q-336358" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(-4,3,5)$ and is parallel to the vectors ${v}_{1}=⟨2,-3,-6⟩$ and ${v}_{2}=⟨0,2,-8⟩.$\n\n{{field-1}}\n$x\\,+$\n{{field-2}}\n$y\\,+\\,z\\,+$\n{{field-3}}\n$=0$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $p=⟨-4,3,5⟩.$\n\nNow, we need to find the normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={v}_{1}\\times {v}_{2} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]\\times [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -3 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -8\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =36i+16j+4k \\\\  & =⟨36,16,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨36,16,4⟩ & =⟨-4,3,5⟩\\cdot ⟨36,16,4⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨36,16,4⟩ & =-76\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨36,16,4⟩ & =-76 \\\\ 36x+16y+4z & =-76 \\\\ 36x+16y+4z+76 & =0 \\\\ 9x+4y+z+19 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388362 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0efe5b11-2c87-58e9-bf7c-e1eecb07adf2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of the Plane Parallel To Two Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d521f88d-c349-5662-9b3d-8810f07d2e73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388364}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d521f88d-c349-5662-9b3d-8810f07d2e73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388364} :answer-field/id #uuid "91214efe-03ea-54ad-9046-a10ee5a63a51" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388363} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ef894fe3-ca66-52ca-ae79-90f2a4fdbf02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388366}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef894fe3-ca66-52ca-ae79-90f2a4fdbf02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388366} :answer-field/id #uuid "95601e1b-6cd9-5a62-862d-4fa5135767fa" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388365} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66c8837d-e954-5680-aee2-3fa8b3413815" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388368}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66c8837d-e954-5680-aee2-3fa8b3413815" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388368} :answer-field/id #uuid "4df92c6a-73ad-5228-bc27-984d9e00aae0" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388367}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "736653a1-5df9-5c46-8da4-cc6725fd4151" :question/math-academy-id "q-336450" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(2,-4,3)$ and is parallel to the vectors ${v}_{1}=⟨1,-3,-2⟩$ and ${v}_{2}=⟨0,4,-1⟩.$\n\n{{field-1}}\n$x\\,+\\,y\\,+$\n{{field-2}} \n$z\\,-$\n{{field-3}}\n$=0$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $p=⟨2,-4,3⟩.$\n\nNow, we need to find the normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={v}_{1}\\times {v}_{2} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\times [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =11i+1j+4k \\\\  & =⟨11,1,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨11,1,4⟩ & =⟨2,-4,3⟩\\cdot ⟨11,1,4⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨11,1,4⟩ & =30\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨11,1,4⟩ & =30 \\\\ 11x+y+4z & =30 \\\\ 11x+y+4z-30 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388369 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b34272b7-a7ad-5a58-974e-54cca957d06e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of the Plane Perpendicular To a Given Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8101687d-7ccb-52d3-a02f-0c5359b90ea1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+z-5=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388371} {:answer/id #uuid "59f14b86-3fe8-52f4-b9c8-dbbfe3669e38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y+3z-5=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388372} {:answer/id #uuid "8b2fcc60-90c3-5609-bfed-6442fa453c39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+2y+z-5=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388373} {:answer/id #uuid "cf820b1a-e763-5ae9-a87b-16e8093772f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+3z-5=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388374} {:answer/id #uuid "168ede94-970d-55eb-9df2-b1ae2213784b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-5y+z=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388375}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8101687d-7ccb-52d3-a02f-0c5359b90ea1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+z-5=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388371} :answer-field/id #uuid "00a3b9d1-0ad0-5d22-99a4-a2440d1b0130" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388370}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "20f01ed6-ba75-58b7-8c4e-708e14bec166" :question/math-academy-id "q-33926" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(1,0,3)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $n=⟨2,0,1⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $p=⟨1,0,3⟩$ and the normal vector of the plane is $n=⟨2,0,1⟩.$\n\nUsing the dot product we can find an equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨2,0,1⟩ & =⟨1,0,3⟩\\cdot ⟨2,0,1⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨2,0,1⟩ & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨2,0,1⟩ & =5 \\\\ 2x+z & =5 \\\\ 2x+z-5 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388376 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b34272b7-a7ad-5a58-974e-54cca957d06e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of the Plane Perpendicular To a Given Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97db5536-94e6-57c9-932b-b7d1dd8ab302" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-y+2z-5=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388378} {:answer/id #uuid "44dca2f2-e0dc-50b3-8855-d2bc9b7b3045" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y+3z+8=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388379} {:answer/id #uuid "d9153e5f-3cdd-5dc5-8a10-ed35868113d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y+3z+6=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388380} {:answer/id #uuid "eaf6de68-de37-58f0-a72d-0d0b902321de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-x+y-2z+6=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388381} {:answer/id #uuid "862e7072-e0fd-5084-b3f3-06155608d474" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y+3z+5=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388382}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d9153e5f-3cdd-5dc5-8a10-ed35868113d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y+3z+6=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388380} :answer-field/id #uuid "bfd31e76-57ed-504c-b930-3502ff9a51eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388377}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c9388b78-08c8-50f9-9be6-ffeb2395d72f" :question/math-academy-id "q-70805" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(-1,1,-2)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $n=i+j+3k.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $p=⟨-1,1,-2⟩$ and the normal vector of the plane is $n=⟨1,1,3⟩.$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨1,1,3⟩ & =⟨-1,1,-2⟩\\cdot ⟨1,1,3⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨1,1,3⟩ & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨1,1,3⟩ & =-6 \\\\ x+y+3z & =-6 \\\\ x+y+3z+6 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388383 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0efe5b11-2c87-58e9-bf7c-e1eecb07adf2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of the Plane Parallel To Two Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dd9289be-1a23-5a8b-ba5b-6add7ee37d78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+3y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388385} {:answer/id #uuid "790d0521-874e-51d0-b6c6-419d062acbdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+3y-4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388386} {:answer/id #uuid "3375216c-42e6-53d5-be8b-f25e9aa93adf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x-2y-4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388387} {:answer/id #uuid "ba184088-3a8d-5f27-97fa-c44f5254289e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z-4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388388} {:answer/id #uuid "a6fa644d-baf4-57e2-8fb4-c652eb6627ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-3z+4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388389}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "790d0521-874e-51d0-b6c6-419d062acbdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+3y-4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388386} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b30b340-ce9e-5a13-9829-adb043ec4a83" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388384}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6822fa82-3903-5a2d-867d-04dce53d85c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-70833" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(2,0,-3)$ and is parallel to the vectors ${v}_{1}=⟨-3,2,0⟩$ and ${v}_{2}=⟨0,0,1⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $p=⟨2,0,-3⟩.$\n\nNow, we need to find the normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={v}_{1}\\times {v}_{2} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\times [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =2i+3j \\\\  & =⟨2,3,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨2,3,0⟩ & =⟨2,0,-3⟩\\cdot ⟨2,3,0⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨2,3,0⟩ & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨2,3,0⟩ & =4 \\\\ 2x+3y & =4 \\\\ 2x+3y-4 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
